Inequality: panels tile by period, real coins, and a technical copy pass

Charlie's round, in order.

- THE COINS ARE COINS. Each has a struck face, a rim per coin down the side
  wall and an inner ring, in three metals with their own face and edge tints
  for light and dark. Flat ellipses read as lozenges.

- PANELS NOW TILE TIME, and the first stops where the second starts. They were
  cut by SOURCE, which overlaps: dig sites run to 1970, tax registers from
  1283, national accounts from 1800. Cut by PERIOD they cannot overlap, and
  each panel simply holds whatever evidence falls inside it. Eleven of the 63
  dig sites sit after 1283 - Cahokia, Mayapan, Tenochtitlan, the !Kung San -
  all outside Europe where no tax register exists, so they move into the later
  panels rather than being dropped to make the boundary tidy.

- "Why are there dots and lines for the middle section" - because I had put
  Alfani's regional series over the top of the towns as lines. They are point
  estimates every fifty years, not a series, so they are dots now like
  everything else, hollow to say "a whole region rather than one town". They
  are also the only evidence for Holland and Flanders, so they could not just
  be cut. One panel, one kind of mark, one summary line.

- Trend lines: a rolling median of nine consecutive observations, one per
  evidence type and never across two. Named as what it is in the legend.

- Real dated ticks inside every panel, off a 1/2/5 ladder so a 10,000-year
  panel and a 200-year one both get years a reader recognises. One range
  caption told you nothing about where inside it a dot sat.

- "All countries" in the picker: drops the highlighted line, keeps the band.

- Copy: "Dug-up houses" is "Excavated settlements", the Gini blurb gives the
  actual definition rather than a gloss, and every layer note now says what is
  measured, over what population, from what record.

- TODO D9: two more charts, both from fields already in the payload and never
  plotted. Gini against Kohler's political-scale coding rises monotonically
  from 0.17 for family-level societies to 0.38 for states, which is this
  site's two subjects in one chart. Old World 0.35 against New World 0.25 is
  his headline finding. Also records what is NOT available: ten OWID slugs for
  wealth-to-income and inheritance flows, all 404.

Adds scripts/coin_animation_options.py, which renders six candidate hover
animations to an animated GIF, because an animation cannot be judged from a
description.
